    slug: 'the-autumn-of-the-patriarch',
    intro: "Gabriel García Márquez's The Autumn of the Patriarch (1975) is a standalone novel about an unnamed Caribbean dictator, possibly more than two hundred years old, found dead in his palace at the novel's opening, told through six long chapters of twisting, tumbling prose. It is García Márquez's most technically demanding novel and his most sustained portrait of absolute power.",
    startWith: 'The Autumn of the Patriarch',
    books: [
      { title: 'The Autumn of the Patriarch', author: 'Gabriel García Márquez', year: 1975, note: 'The complete novel — the General\'s centuries of despotism, told in six unbroken chapters. Stand-alone.' },
      { title: 'One Hundred Years of Solitude', author: 'Gabriel García Márquez', year: 1967, note: 'The Buendía family over seven generations — García Márquez\'s masterpiece and the essential companion to The Autumn of the Patriarch.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'The General in His Labyrinth', author: 'Gabriel García Márquez', year: 1989, note: 'Simón Bolívar\'s last river journey — García Márquez returns to the theme of power, age, and death with a historical subject.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'I the Supreme', author: 'Augusto Roa Bastos', year: 1974, note: 'The supreme dictator of Paraguay dictates to a secretary — the parallel Latin American novel about absolute power, as technically demanding as Patriarch.', isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Is The Autumn of the Patriarch related to One Hundred Years of Solitude?', a: 'They are not related narratively — different characters, different country, different time period, and the patriarch\'s unnamed Caribbean nation is not Macondo. But they are clearly companion works, written by the same imagination about the same preoccupations: the relationship between time, power, and Latin American history; the mythologizing of political figures; the role of solitude in the lives of powerful men. García Márquez has said he always thought of the two novels as parts of the same larger project about Caribbean political reality. Most readers come to The Autumn of the Patriarch after One Hundred Years of Solitude.' },
      { q: 'Why is The Autumn of the Patriarch written in such long sentences?', a: 'The novel\'s famous prose style — sentences that extend for pages, with clauses piling on clauses, perspectives shifting mid-sentence between multiple narrators — is a formal enactment of the subject. Under a dictatorship, all voices are eventually absorbed into the dictator\'s voice; the novel\'s syntax performs this absorption, beginning sentences in the voices of those who discover the body and gradually swallowing those voices into the General\'s own consciousness. The long sentences also enact the novel\'s preoccupation with time and its distortion under power: in a dictatorship, time itself becomes subject to decree. García Márquez worked on the novel for eight years, and the prose style was the last thing to arrive.' },
      { q: 'How old is the patriarch in The Autumn of the Patriarch?', a: 'His age is never definitively stated, and this ambiguity is intentional. The patriarch has ruled for so long that no one can remember a time before him — he is variously described as between 107 and 232 years old, and the novel does not resolve this. His indeterminate age is part of the novel\'s argument: under absolute power, ordinary chronology breaks down. The patriarch\'s own sense of time is distorted — he genuinely does not know how old he is. His longevity also serves as a figure for the persistence of certain kinds of political power in Latin American history, which has seemed to renew itself endlessly in different forms.' },
    ],
