    slug: 'brighton-rock',
    intro: "Graham Greene's Brighton Rock (1938) is a standalone novel following Pinkie Brown, a seventeen-year-old Catholic gang leader in Brighton, who commits a murder and then marries a young woman who witnessed his crime in order to silence her. Greene's first major novel of the tension between Catholicism, evil, and grace — the book that established his mature voice and his preoccupation with damnation.",
    startWith: 'Brighton Rock',
    books: [
      { title: 'Brighton Rock', author: 'Graham Greene', year: 1938, note: 'Pinkie\'s murder, his marriage, and Ida Arnold\'s pursuit — complete standalone. The 1947 film with Richard Attenborough as Pinkie is a classic of British noir.' },
      { title: 'The Power and the Glory', author: 'Graham Greene', year: 1940, note: 'A "whisky priest" flees a revolutionary government in Mexico — Greene\'s most celebrated novel; the Catholic preoccupation of Brighton Rock here given its fullest treatment.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'The Heart of the Matter', author: 'Graham Greene', year: 1948, note: 'A British police officer in West Africa during World War Two is destroyed by his own pity — Greene\'s most psychologically penetrating novel of Catholic guilt.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'Our Man in Havana', author: 'Graham Greene', year: 1958, note: 'A vacuum cleaner salesman in Cuba invents imaginary agents for British intelligence — Greene\'s most comic novel, a deliberate change of register from the Catholic tragedies.', isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What is the relationship between Brighton Rock and Catholicism?', a: 'Brighton Rock is Greene\'s first explicitly Catholic novel — the beginning of a preoccupation with damnation, grace, and the boundaries of salvation that would run through The Power and the Glory, The Heart of the Matter, and The End of the Affair. Pinkie is a Catholic (however lapsed and however twisted his understanding of the faith), and the novel\'s argument — stated most clearly by the priest at the end — is that between the damned and the saved there is a difference too profound for human moral categories to capture; that even Pinkie\'s damnation may contain a grace inaccessible to Ida\'s secular moralism. This is a genuinely difficult theological position, and it makes Brighton Rock a more disturbing novel than a straightforward condemnation of Pinkie would be.' },
      { q: 'Who is Ida Arnold and why does she matter?', a: 'Ida Arnold — the buxom, cheerful, secular woman who pursues Pinkie out of her own sense of fair play, not out of any religious conviction — is one of the novel\'s most important characters precisely because she represents everything Greene is arguing against. Ida believes in Right and Wrong, in human justice, in the ordinary moral calculus of secular life. Greene presents her as sympathetic (she is genuinely kind, genuinely committed to justice) but ultimately shallow: her moral universe has no room for the theological categories (sin, damnation, grace, salvation) that Pinkie and Rose (whom Pinkie marries) inhabit, however disfigured. Ida is the novel\'s representative reader, and her inadequacy is partly the novel\'s critique of the reader.' },
      { q: 'Is Brighton Rock a crime novel or a Catholic novel?', a: 'It is both simultaneously, which is what makes it exceptional. Greene was working in the tradition of the English crime novel (his debt to Eric Ambler and others is clear in the Brighton setting and the gang plot), but he was using crime fiction as the vehicle for a theological argument that the genre had never previously been asked to carry. The crime plot — Pinkie\'s murder of Hale, his marriage to Rose to silence her, Ida\'s pursuit — is entirely functional as a thriller. But the theological weight Greene puts on Pinkie\'s Catholicism transforms the conventional criminal into a figure of damnation in the Augustinian sense, and his crimes become something other than crimes: they become sins, which is a different category entirely.' },
    ],
