    slug: 'shogun',
    intro: "James Clavell's Shōgun (1975) is a standalone epic historical novel set in feudal Japan in the year 1600, following English navigator John Blackthorne, who is shipwrecked on the Japanese coast and becomes entangled in a massive power struggle between rival daimyo. One of the bestselling novels of the twentieth century.",
    startWith: 'Shōgun',
    books: [
      { title: 'Shōgun', author: 'James Clavell', year: 1975, note: 'Blackthorne, Toranaga, Mariko, and the world of feudal Japan. Standalone; no direct sequel.' },
      { title: 'Taikō', author: 'James Clavell', year: 1982, note: 'The story of Toyotomi Hideyoshi — the peasant who rose to become ruler of all Japan — set earlier in the same historical period as Shōgun. Part of Clavell\'s Asian Saga; works well as a standalone.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'Gai-Jin', author: 'James Clavell', year: 1993, note: 'Set in Japan in 1862, two hundred years after Shōgun, following Western merchants and Japanese clans in the early Meiji era. The direct continuation of the Asian Saga\'s Japan thread.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'King Rat', author: 'James Clavell', year: 1962, note: 'Clavell\'s first novel and the most autobiographical — based on his own experience as a prisoner of war in Changi prison camp in Singapore during World War II. A very different book from Shōgun but equally gripping.', isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Is Shōgun historically accurate?', a: 'Shōgun is a work of fiction closely based on documented history. The events of 1600 Japan — the power vacuum after the death of Toyotomi Hideyoshi, the competing factions among the five regents, and the decisive Battle of Sekigahara — are real history, and Clavell follows them closely. The character of Toranaga is based on Tokugawa Ieyasu, who did become shogun and whose descendants ruled Japan until 1868. The character of Blackthorne is based on the real English navigator William Adams, who was shipwrecked in Japan in 1600 and became a valued advisor to Tokugawa. Clavell invented many characters, compressed timelines, and added the love story, but the political framework is accurate. Japanese historians have noted some cultural inaccuracies and Clavell\'s tendency to romanticize feudal Japan, but the basic historical architecture is sound.' },
      { q: 'Is Shōgun part of a series?', a: 'Shōgun is the second book published in Clavell\'s Asian Saga, a loosely connected series of novels set across Asia over several centuries. Despite its placement in the series, Shōgun stands entirely alone — it has no direct narrative connection to the other books, and no prior reading is required. The Asian Saga novels share themes (the encounter between East and West, the dynamics of power and loyalty) and occasionally cross-reference historical events, but each is self-contained. The reading order most often recommended is publication order: King Rat (1962), Tai-Pan (1966), Shōgun (1975), Noble House (1981), Taikō (1982), Gai-Jin (1993). But Shōgun is by far the most acclaimed and most widely read of the six, and many readers begin and end there.' },
      { q: 'What is the 2024 Shōgun TV series?', a: 'The 2024 FX/Hulu adaptation of Shōgun, starring Hiroyuki Sanada as Yoshii Toranaga and Cosmo Jarvis as Blackthorne, won the Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Drama Series and Outstanding Limited or Anthology Series in 2024 — a historic double. The series was praised for its portrayal of Japanese culture and language (a majority of the dialogue is in Japanese with subtitles), for the complexity of its characters (particularly Toranaga, who is given far greater interiority than in the novel), and for the quality of its production. It closely follows the novel\'s plot while developing the Japanese characters more fully than Clavell\'s Blackthorne-centric narrative. Many viewers came to the novel after the series; many novel readers consider the series a worthy adaptation and in some ways an improvement.' },
    ],
