    slug: 'the-prime-of-miss-jean-brodie',
    intro: "Muriel Spark's The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie (1961) is a standalone novel following an unconventional Edinburgh schoolteacher who selects six girls from her class at the Marcia Blaine School to become her 'crème de la crème,' sharing her enthusiasms for Renaissance art, Mussolini, and romantic love — and the girl who will eventually betray her. One of the most perfectly constructed short novels in the English language.",
    startWith: 'The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie',
    books: [
      { title: 'The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie', author: 'Muriel Spark', year: 1961, note: "The complete novel. Entirely standalone." },
      { title: 'The Driver\'s Seat', author: 'Muriel Spark', year: 1970, note: "Lise travelling to a city where she intends to be murdered — Spark's most disturbing novel; shares The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie's precise, cold, manipulative narrative voice and its interest in a woman who exerts extreme control over the circumstances of her own destruction; told prospectively rather than in the retrospective manner of Miss Brodie.", isOptional: true },
      { title: 'Memento Mori', author: 'Muriel Spark', year: 1959, note: "A group of elderly people in London begin receiving anonymous phone calls reminding them that they must die — Spark's third novel; her most comic meditation on mortality; the theological implications of death that are implicit in The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie are here made explicit and comic.", isOptional: true },
      { title: 'The Girls of Slender Means', author: 'Muriel Spark', year: 1963, note: "Young women in a London hostel in the weeks following VE Day — Spark's novel written immediately after The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie and sharing its period setting (the 1940s), its group of women, and its retrospective narrative that reveals a hidden violence at the novel's heart.", isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Who is Jean Brodie, and what makes her such a fascinating character?', a: "Jean Brodie is one of the great antiheroes of twentieth-century British fiction — a woman of genuine vitality, aesthetic sensibility, and pedagogical passion whose idealism is inseparable from a narcissism and a fascination with fascism that have catastrophic consequences for the girls in her care. She is magnetic and dangerous in equal measure: the novel is organized around the reader's dual response to her, admiring her refusal to be ordinary while recognizing the violence of her project. What makes her particularly interesting is that Spark never allows the reader a stable moral position from which to judge her: the girl who eventually betrays her (Sandy Stranger) becomes a renowned psychologist — which suggests that the Brodie experience had significant intellectual value — but Sandy spends her life gripping the bars of her convent cell, which suggests that the encounter with Brodie also caused lasting damage. The novel refuses the comfortable conclusion that Brodie was simply a villain or simply a heroine." },
      { q: 'What is the significance of Jean Brodie\'s fascism?', a: "Miss Brodie's admiration for Mussolini and Franco is the novel's most politically uncomfortable element, and Spark is careful about what she does with it. Brodie's fascism is presented partly as a kind of aesthetic enthusiasm — she admires the order and grandeur of Mussolini's marching troops in the same spirit in which she admires Giotto — and partly as an extension of her general belief that she knows better than the democratic majority what is good for people. Her project with the Brodie Set is itself a kind of fascist pedagogy: she selects, cultivates, and controls her six girls according to her own vision of what they should become, without reference to their own desires or judgment. The most chilling consequence of her fascism is sending one of the girls, Joyce Emily, to fight for Franco in Spain, where she is killed on a train — a death that Miss Brodie never fully acknowledges the responsibility for." },
      { q: 'What is Muriel Spark\'s narrative technique in The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie?', a: "Spark's narrative technique in The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie is one of the most distinctive in twentieth-century fiction. The novel moves freely through time, telling us the future fates of characters before those futures have occurred in the narrative: we learn early in the novel that Sandy Stranger will betray Miss Brodie, that Rose Stanley will become sexually famous, that one of the girls will be killed in Spain. This prospective technique — telling us what will happen before it happens — prevents suspense while creating a different kind of tension: we watch the events unfold knowing their consequences, which gives even the lightest social comedy an air of inevitability and doom. The technique also embodies the novel's theological argument: Spark, a Catholic convert, believed that human lives are shaped by a divine foreknowledge that makes free will and predestination simultaneously real, and the novel's narrative structure enacts that theological position formally." },
    ],
