    slug: 'raphael-lamartine',
    intro: "Raphaël (1849) by Alphonse de Lamartine is an autobiographical novel of doomed love that recounts a version of Lamartine's relationship with Julie Charles, the woman who inspired his most famous poem, 'Le Lac' (1820) — a married invalid whom he met at Aix-les-Bains in 1816, with whom he spent the autumn, and who died the following year before they could meet again, leaving him with only the lake and the memory. Raphaël is the novelistic account of this relationship: the young poet Raphaël meets 'Julie' (named for Rousseau's heroine) at a Swiss spa, they fall in love during a month of extraordinary happiness, she returns to Paris and her dying husband, and she dies leaving only letters and the landscape. The book is highly literary, deliberately echoing La Nouvelle Héloïse and Werther, and is as much a meditation on memory, beauty, and the relationship between lived experience and its transformation into poetry as it is a love story. It appeared in 1849, when Lamartine was a major political figure (he had been effectively head of government in 1848), and its retrospective tenderness reads partly as an escape from the failed politics of the Second Republic into the pure world of youthful feeling.",
