    slug: 'la-petite-fadette',
    intro: "La Petite Fadette (Little Fadette, 1849) by George Sand is the last and most fully achieved of her Berry pastoral trilogy — the story of the strange girl called Fadette (a diminutive of fée, fairy), despised by the village as a witch's granddaughter, who is loved by the twin brother Landry while his other twin Sylvinet pines away from neglect. Sand took the subject of the twins as a study in fraternal jealousy and possessive love: Sylvinet's attachment to Landry is presented as a kind of pathological doubling, a refusal of individual identity that contrasts with Fadette's fiercely individual identity forged in social rejection. La Petite Fadette is the richest of the Berry pastorals in its engagement with folklore: Fadette's reputation as a witch, her knowledge of herbs and cures, and her relationship with the natural world are presented with the same sympathetic precision as the farm practices and seasonal rhythms of the other pastorals, and Sand was one of the first major writers to treat rural women's knowledge and rural folk belief as worthy of literary attention. The novel ends with Fadette's transformation from social outcast to respected villager, achieved through the combination of Landry's love, her own intelligence, and the fortune she inherits from her grandmother — a resolution that Sand presents not as a fairy-tale but as the social recognition of what was always real.",
