    slug: 'la-chronique-de-charles-ix',
    intro: "La Chronique du règne de Charles IX (Chronicle of the Reign of Charles IX, 1829) by Prosper Mérimée is his only novel and one of the most important historical novels in the French tradition — set during the wars of religion and centered on the events of the Saint Bartholomew's Day Massacre of 1572, in which Catherine de Médicis ordered the slaughter of Huguenot leaders and thousands of Protestant civilians. The novel follows two brothers, the Catholic Comte de Mergy and the Protestant Bernard de Mergy, on opposite sides of the religious divide, and its most remarkable feature is its refusal of the heroic mode of historical fiction: where Scott's historical novels and Hugo's Notre-Dame de Paris treat the historical world as a setting for moral clarity and individual greatness, Mérimée's chronicle is interested in the confusion, the contingency, and the moral ambiguity of the events. The massacre is not presented as a tragedy of religious persecution but as a moment of collective psychosis in which individual human beings make terrible decisions in a context they do not control and cannot understand. The tone is ironic, the narration is cold, and the novel anticipates by several decades the Naturalist understanding of historical events as products of social and biological forces rather than individual choices.",
