    intro: "Tamango (1829) by Prosper Mérimée is one of the most challenging short stories in the French canon — the story of a slave ship and the slave rebellion led by an African chief named Tamango who was responsible for selling his own people into slavery. The story begins with the slave trade in Africa: the French captain Ledoux purchases 160 slaves, including Tamango himself (who was drunk when he sold his wife Ayché into the bargain), and sets sail for the Caribbean. Tamango leads a rebellion that kills most of the crew, but the surviving slaves cannot navigate the ship, and the story ends with the ship drifting until most of the slaves die and a British frigate rescues the survivors — including Tamango, who is taken to Jamaica, freed, joins a regimental band, and dies of drink. Mérimée's characteristic cold narrative method — the refusal to moralize, the anthropological distance, the presentation of horror without commentary — is here applied to the most horrifying material of his century, with an effect that remains deeply unsettling: the story neither condemns slavery nor endorses it, but describes the system and its effects with a precision that amounts to a more disturbing indictment than any moral commentary could provide.",
