    slug: 'le-grand-meaulnes',
    intro: "Le Grand Meaulnes (The Lost Estate, 1913) by Alain-Fournier (Henri-Alban Fournier, killed in action 1914) is the only novel Alain-Fournier completed, and one of the best-loved works in the French tradition — a novel of adolescent enchantment, loss, and the impossibility of recovering a magical world once it has been left behind. The narrator, François Seurel, is the son of the village schoolmaster in Sologne in the 1890s; his best friend is the charismatic, reckless Augustin Meaulnes; and the novel's central event is Meaulnes's accidental discovery of a strange estate (Le Domaine sans nom) where a child's wedding feast is being prepared, a moment of enchantment that he can never return to and that Seurel narrates as both observer and participant. Le Grand Meaulnes belongs to the tradition of the bildungsroman, but its distinctive quality is its treatment of enchantment: the magical world Meaulnes discovers is not described as fantastic but as the ordinary world seen at an extraordinary angle, and the novel's melancholy lies in the impossibility of returning to the angle of vision that made it magical. The novel was an immediate success and has remained continuously in print; it is the most read French school text of the twentieth century (often read alongside Hugo and Flaubert), and its influence on the French tradition of the lost paradise is pervasive.",
