    slug: 'la-fille-elisa-goncourt',
    intro: "La Fille Élisa (1877) by Edmond de Goncourt — the first novel he published after Jules's death in 1870 — is his most politically controversial work, a study of a prostitute convicted of murder who is sentenced to the cellular prison system (the 'silent system,' derived from American Quaker penitentiaries, in which prisoners were kept in permanent solitary confinement without being allowed to speak) and who is destroyed by the silence before she can be released. The novel is both a character study of Élisa (her childhood poverty, her entry into prostitution, the murder, the trial) and a political pamphlet against the silent system: Edmond de Goncourt researched the novel by visiting the Saint-Lazare women's prison and interviewing officials, and the final third of the novel is a documentary indictment of penal silence as a method of psychological destruction that the novel presents as worse than the crime it was meant to address. La Fille Élisa is Edmond de Goncourt's most politically direct novel and the one that most clearly places him in the tradition of the Naturalist exposé that Zola's preface to Germinie Lacerteux had defined: the novel as a social document demanding reform. The novel sold well — it was a succès de scandale — and contributed to the parliamentary discussion of prison reform that produced the 1891 Bérenger Law's limitation of the silent system's scope.",
    startWith: "Start with Germinie Lacerteux (1864, with Jules) for the Goncourts' founding Naturalist method, then read La Fille Élisa for its most politically direct application. Sœur Philomène (1861) provides the institutional-study companion to La Fille Élisa's prison world.",
