    slug: 'l-oeuvre-zola',
    intro: "L'Œuvre (1886) by Émile Zola is the fourteenth volume of Les Rougon-Macquart and the most directly autobiographical in its treatment of the artistic milieu — the story of the painter Claude Lantier, who has glimpsed a new kind of painting (clearly Impressionism, though Zola never names it), whose ambition exceeds his technical capacity, and who is destroyed by his inability to realize on canvas what he sees with his imagination. The novel is roman à clef in the tradition of the Bildungsroman: Claude is partly Cézanne (Zola's childhood friend, whose departure from Paris to Aix-en-Provence after the novel's publication ended their friendship permanently), partly Manet, and partly Zola himself at his moments of doubt about his own method. The novel ends with Claude hanging himself in front of the unfinished monumental canvas that has consumed his life, a painting that may or may not be a masterpiece — Zola deliberately prevents the reader from knowing — and his friend the writer Sandoz (the most directly autobiographical figure) attending the funeral and reflecting on the waste of a life given to the ideal of art. L'Œuvre is also the most sustained fictional account of the Impressionist movement in French literature — the salons, the studios, the critical debates, the friendship networks and rivalries of the 1860s-1880s Paris art world are documented with the same research precision Zola brings to coal mines and markets.",
    startWith: "Start with L'Œuvre itself — it is self-contained. Readers who want Claude Lantier's earlier appearances should read Le Ventre de Paris (volume 3) for his first significant scene. Germinal (volume 13) and La Bête humaine (volume 17) provide the range of the cycle that L'Œuvre's artistic focus complements.",
