    slug: 'madeleine-ferat',
    intro: "Madeleine Férat (1868) by Émile Zola is his second novel and the most direct predecessor of the Rougon-Macquart method — the first systematic application of hereditary and physiological theory to a fictional narrative, adapted from the biologist Prosper Lucas's theory of 'impregnation': the idea that a woman's first sexual partner permanently marks her physiology, so that any children she subsequently bears will resemble him rather than her later husband. Zola takes this theory as a structural premise: Madeleine, who had a lover before her marriage to Guillaume, finds that her daughter by Guillaume resembles the first lover so strongly that Guillaume cannot ignore the evidence of what Madeleine's past contains. The novel is Zola's first extended exercise in what would become the Rougon-Macquart framework — the inherited and physiological determination of character — and the way it uses the pseudo-scientific theory of impregnation as a plot mechanism shows both the promise and the limitations of the Naturalist method in its early form. Madeleine Férat is rarely read now, but it is the essential document of Zola's transition from the Romantic confessional mode of La Confession de Claude (1865) to the Naturalist scientific framework of the Rougon-Macquart, and the most important of his early works for understanding how the mature method developed.",
