    slug: 'son-ventre-de-paris',
    intro: "Le Ventre de Paris (1873) by Émile Zola is the third volume of Les Rougon-Macquart and the most formally ambitious novel of its first phase — a study of the Central Market (Les Halles) of Paris as a complete social, economic, and sensory world, in which the presence of the food, its abundance, its smells, its colors, and its constant movement functions as a sustained symbolic argument about the relationship between the fat (les gras) and the thin (les maigres) in Second Empire society. The protagonist Florent, a Republican exile returning from Cayenne after the 1851 coup, is the incarnation of the 'thin' — he is surrounded by and ultimately destroyed by the abundance he cannot participate in, the market's cheerful prosperity that is built on the political crime that exiled him. Zola's great achievement in Le Ventre de Paris is the sensory prose — the chapters devoted to the cheeses, the fish, the charcuterie, the vegetables, each rendered with a precision and sensuous detail that makes the food itself a political statement about what the Second Empire's prosperity means and who is excluded from it. The novel is also notable for its satirical portrait of the bourgeois artist's relationship to social comfort: Claude Lantier (ancestor of the painter in L'Œuvre) appears for the first time, and his aestheticization of the market while his political ally is being destroyed by it is Zola's ironic comment on art's capacity for political disengagement.",
