    slug: 'une-page-damour-zola',
    intro: "Une page d'amour (1878) by Émile Zola is the eighth volume of Les Rougon-Macquart and the most intimate — a deliberately restricted study of a widow's adulterous love for her doctor, set almost entirely in the bourgeois Passy neighborhood of Paris, with Paris itself as a constant backdrop that Zola renders in a series of tableaux at different hours and in different weathers. The novel is unusual in the cycle for its domestic quietness: there is no social panorama, no industrial or commercial setting, no collective struggle. Instead, Zola takes the most private emotional situation — a woman's suppressed desire for a man she knows she cannot love without destroying everything she has — and follows it with the same systematic precision he applies elsewhere to coal mines and market halls. Hélène Mouret (the Macquart line, carrying the hereditary nervous instability) is Zola's most careful portrait of a woman whose desire exists entirely in the interior — she represses it, sublimates it, watches it destroy her daughter Jeanne anyway through a mechanism of psychosomatic illness that Zola presents as the consequence of the emotional suppression. Une page d'amour is the Rougon-Macquart volume that most directly anticipates the psychological novel of the 1890s and that has the most in common with Maupassant's Fort comme la mort and with the fiction of the repressed desire that Bourget and France also practised.",
