    slug: 'la-confession-de-claude',
    intro: "La Confession de Claude (1865) by Émile Zola is his first novel and the most autobiographical — a semi-confessional first-person account of a young man's attempt to reform a Parisian prostitute through love, and his failure. Written when Zola was twenty-five and drawing directly on his own experience in Paris, the novel belongs to the tradition of the Romantic confessional novel (Musset's Confession d'un enfant du siècle, Chateaubriand's René, Constant's Adolphe) more than to the Naturalist method Zola would develop in the next decade. The young protagonist Claude takes the prostitute Laurence into his household and attempts through patient love to make her into a respectable woman; she remains what she is, not through vice but through the determinism of her circumstances and character that the mature Zola would explain through hereditary theory, and Claude's failure is both personal and aesthetic — the failure of the Romantic project of redemption through love. La Confession de Claude is remarkable chiefly as the document of Zola's literary origins: the author of Germinal and L'Assommoir begins in the confessional Romantic tradition he will spend the next twenty years systematically dismantling, and the distance between La Confession de Claude and the mature Rougon-Macquart is the measure of what Naturalism replaced.",
    startWith: "Start with La Confession de Claude as a historical document if you want to understand Zola's development. Most readers will prefer to begin with a Rougon-Macquart novel — L'Assommoir or Germinal — and come back to La Confession de Claude as an interesting early work after they know the mature method.",
