    slug: 'son-excellence-rougon',
    intro: "Son Excellence Eugène Rougon (1876) by Émile Zola is the sixth volume of Les Rougon-Macquart and the most direct study of Second Empire political power in the cycle — the rise, fall, and restoration of a Bonapartist minister, Eugène Rougon (first seen in La Fortune des Rougon), through the court politics, parliamentary intrigues, and personal favors of Napoleon III's regime in the 1850s and 1860s. Zola's research for the novel was the most systematic of his career to that point: he spent months in the archives studying Second Empire parliamentary debates and interviewed surviving officials and politicians. The resulting novel is the French Naturalist equivalent of a political insider's memoir, showing the mechanics of power — the exchange of favors, the making and breaking of careers, the role of sexuality in political patronage, the relationship between the powerful and their clients — with a precision that no French novelist before or since has quite matched. Eugène Rougon himself is one of Zola's most fully realized characters: a man of enormous capability who uses his intelligence entirely in the service of political survival, and whose relationship with the ambitious Clorinde Balbi (modeled on the historical Countess of Castiglione) drives the novel's central intrigue.",
