    slug: 'la-joie-de-vivre-zola',
    intro: "La Joie de vivre (1884) by Émile Zola is the twelfth volume of Les Rougon-Macquart and the most philosophically pessimistic — a systematic engagement with Schopenhauer's philosophy of suffering, mediated through the contrast between the generous, life-affirming Pauline Quenu and the hypochondriac, self-pitying Lazare Chanteau who is destroyed by his own intelligence and his awareness of death. The novel is set in a Norman fishing village on the coast, and the sea is Zola's central symbol: it destroys with indifferent regularity, swallowing fishermen and eroding the coastline, while the human community organizes its life around the constant fact of loss. Pauline, who has inherited money from her family and uses it to support the Chanteau household through its economic and personal crises, is Zola's portrait of what the philosophy of joy-in-life would look like if it were embodied — and Lazare's deterioration, as his grandiose schemes fail and his fear of death consumes him, is the counterportrait of what Schopenhauer saw as the typical human condition. La Joie de vivre is Zola's most directly autobiographical novel (Lazare's anxiety about death was Zola's own) and the one that most directly engages with the philosophical debates of the 1880s about pessimism, optimism, and the will to live.",
    startWith: "Start with La Joie de vivre itself — it is entirely self-contained. Germinal (the next volume, 13) provides the most dramatic contrast: where La Joie de vivre is set in a small family and engaged with philosophical pessimism, Germinal is set in a mass and engaged with collective political action.",
