    slug: 'pot-bouille',
    intro: "Pot-Bouille (1882) by Émile Zola is the tenth volume of Les Rougon-Macquart and the most savage satire of bourgeois domesticity in French fiction — the story of the young provincial Octave Mouret's arrival in Paris and his installation in a prosperous Haussmann-era apartment building whose respectable facade conceals a seething interior of adultery, hypocrisy, social ambition, and systematic exploitation of servant women. The title — 'pot-bouille' means the cheap everyday stew of domestic life, as opposed to the grand cuisine performed for guests — announces the novel's thesis: that the bourgeois household is a performance of respectability maintained by and for the social gaze, while the actual domestic reality is squalor, lust, and mutual exploitation. Zola's method is panoramic: he takes every apartment in the building as a stage, populates each with a different bourgeois configuration (the pious but faithless couple, the social-climbing newcomers, the progressive doctor who is privately as hypocritical as his neighbors), and shows them all participating in the same system. The servants' staircase is Zola's mordant counter-structure: on the servants' stairs, where no one is watching, the same respectable residents expose their actual behavior. Pot-Bouille is the companion and prequel to Au Bonheur des Dames (1883), which follows Octave Mouret into his great commercial enterprise — together they form Zola's fullest portrait of Second Empire bourgeois society in ascent.",
