    slug: 'la-bete-humaine',
    intro: "La Bête humaine (1890) by Émile Zola is the seventeenth volume of Les Rougon-Macquart and the darkest — a novel of violence, sexual compulsion, and murder set against the backdrop of the new railway network, in which the driver of the express train between Paris and Le Havre, Jacques Lantier, is possessed by an atavistic urge to kill women that he must struggle to control. The 'human beast' of the title is not merely Jacques but the compulsion itself — what Zola presents, through the language of hereditary determinism, as the ancestral violence that civilization has incompletely suppressed, resurfacing in the modern industrial world with new velocity and power. The railway is Zola's central symbol: the locomotive La Lison, which Jacques tends with a devotion approaching love, is the most sustained mechanical symbol in the Rougon-Macquart — the iron beast that moves through the landscape with inhuman energy and that Jacques can control only temporarily before the violence inside him finds its expression. The novel interweaves three murder plots: a bourgeois couple's conspiracy, Jacques's own struggle with his compulsion, and the final catastrophe in which the engine runs without a driver through the dark toward a battlefield. La Bête humaine is one of the great French naturalist novels and the most direct influence on the roman noir and crime fiction tradition that followed.",
