    slug: 'l-argent-zola',
    intro: "L'Argent (1891) by Émile Zola is the eighteenth volume of Les Rougon-Macquart, the great novel of financial capitalism and the stock exchange, and the companion volume to Nana and Pot-Bouille for the most comprehensive fictional portrait of the Second Empire at its most expansive and corrupt. The novel follows Aristide Saccard, a speculator who appeared in La Curée (1872), as he founds a new bank — the Banque Universelle — and drives it through a cycle of artificial inflation, speculative mania, ruin, and collapse that mirrors the boom-and-bust cycles of nineteenth-century capitalism. Zola's subject is money itself as a force — not a tool but a dynamic system with its own momentum, capable of generating apparent prosperity while accumulating the conditions of its own destruction. The research for L'Argent was as thorough as the research for Germinal: Zola spent weeks in the Paris Bourse, studying the mechanics of stock speculation, the social architecture of the exchange floor, and the jargon of finance, then transmuted this research into the novel's most celebrated set pieces — the trading-floor scenes during the rise and collapse of Saccard's bank. L'Argent is also notable for its treatment of the parallel between financial speculation and sexual conquest: Saccard's ability to generate belief and excitement in his investors is the same talent he exercises over women, and both forms of seduction end in the same ruin.",
