    slug: 'la-terre-zola',
    intro: "La Terre (1887) by Émile Zola is the fifteenth volume of Les Rougon-Macquart, the most brutal of the cycle, and the most radical extension of Naturalist method to a subject — the French peasantry — that had been largely romanticized by the literary tradition. Set in the Beauce wheat plain south of Paris, La Terre follows the Fouan family through a cycle of violence, greed, and dispossession triggered when the patriarch Old Fouan divides his land among his children, only to find that the division transforms them from family into predators. Zola's thesis is that the ownership of land, in the peasant world, activates the same primitive compulsions that the Rougon-Macquart traces throughout the social hierarchy — the inheritance dispute, the family murder, the dispossession of the old by the young — and that the idealization of the peasant by Romantic literature is a systematic falsification of what life on the land actually produces in human character. The reception of La Terre was scandalized: eight naturalist writers published a manifesto against the novel's excessiveness (the 'Manifeste des Cinq'), and the controversy helped accelerate the movement away from Naturalism toward the Symbolist and psychological approaches that dominated the 1890s. La Terre remains the most controversial of the Rougon-Macquart volumes and the most deliberately confrontational.",
    startWith: "Start with Germinal (1885) before La Terre — Germinal establishes Zola's capacity for compassionate as well as brutal Naturalism, and La Terre makes the most sense in the context of the full cycle's commitment to showing each class in its own terms. La Terre can be read alone but benefits from the comparison.",
