    intro: "Thaïs (1890) by Anatole France is the most perfect example of his ironic philosophical method: a short novel set in fourth-century Alexandria, in which the ascetic monk Paphnutius sets out to convert the famous courtesan Thaïs and bring her to a life of holiness, only to find that his own desire for her, suppressed beneath the zeal of his mission, destroys him as surely as it had destroyed the men he was saving her from. France's irony operates simultaneously on several levels: the story is historically sourced (there was a historical saint Thais), the theology is accurate (the desert fathers' theology of desire and sublimation is rendered precisely), and the comedy is devastating (Paphnutius is the victim of the very mechanism of repression that he imposes on Thaïs). The novel was read on publication as an anti-clerical satire — France was the leading figure of the anti-clerical literary intellectual tradition of the Third Republic — but its psychological precision transcends satire: Paphnutius is not merely the butt of an anti-religious joke but a genuinely tragic figure whose self-destruction follows from the very qualities that make him admirable. Massenet's opera Thaïs (1894), which includes the famous Méditation, is the immediate musical adaptation and gives some sense of how the novel was read in its own time — not as polemical satire but as a tragic love story told with exceptional elegance and moral complexity.",
