    slug: 'la-bas-huysmans',
    intro: "Là-Bas (Down There, 1891) by Joris-Karl Huysmans is the novel in which Huysmans moves from the Decadent aestheticism of À Rebours toward the Catholicism that would eventually absorb him completely — a study of Satanism and the occult in contemporary Paris, intertwined with a historical account of Gilles de Rais, the fifteenth-century marshal of France who was convicted of the murder of hundreds of children in the practice of Satanism. The protagonist, Durtal, is a novelist working on a biography of Gilles de Rais (exactly the biography that Huysmans was writing while composing Là-Bas) who becomes simultaneously drawn into the contemporary Parisian Satanist underground through his affair with Mme. Chantelouve and fascinated by the question of whether medieval Satanism expressed a genuine spiritual reality that the positivist and materialist world of the Third Republic was unable to account for. Là-Bas is therefore at once a novel about the occult world of 1890s Paris (the Rosicrucians, the Martinists, the practitioners of black magic and Satanic masses who were a genuine feature of Parisian intellectual life at the end of the century), a historical novel about Gilles de Rais and the fifteenth-century world of Joan of Arc, and a spiritual autobiography in which Huysmans-as-Durtal begins the journey toward Catholicism by recognizing that the occult world proves the existence of spiritual reality that positivism denies.",
