    slug: 'en-route-huysmans',
    intro: "En Route (1895) by Joris-Karl Huysmans is the central text of his spiritual autobiography — the novel in which Durtal (the protagonist of Là-Bas, now understood as Huysmans's autobiographical surrogate) undergoes the conversion to Catholicism that Huysmans himself experienced in 1892, retreating to the Trappist monastery of Notre-Dame de Fontgombault and discovering in Gregorian chant, the liturgy of the Mass, and the atmosphere of the cloister the spiritual reality he had been seeking in Decadence and the occult. En Route is at once a spiritual autobiography (the most important Catholic conversion narrative in French literature), a work of liturgical aesthetics (Huysmans's account of Gregorian chant and the different chants of the liturgical year is the most influential piece of writing about plainchant produced in the late nineteenth century), and a sustained meditation on the relationship between beauty and truth — the argument that the aesthetic experience of the liturgy is not merely a sensory pleasure but a genuine form of spiritual knowledge. Huysmans's conversion was a scandal in the literary world: he had been the leading figure of French Decadence, and his turn to Catholicism was read by many of his contemporaries as an act of betrayal or madness.",
    startWith: "En Route (1895) — the novel is long (about 400 pages) and requires Là-Bas as preparation; it is the third of the Durtal tetralogy. The W.S. Fleming English translation (1896) exists in reprints; no modern English translation exists. The best approach is to read À Rebours, Là-Bas, and En Route in sequence to follow the spiritual trajectory from aestheticism through occultism to Catholicism.",
