    slug: 'la-cathedrale-huysmans',
    intro: "La Cathédrale (The Cathedral, 1898) by Joris-Karl Huysmans is the novel in which Huysmans-as-Durtal arrives at the spiritual destination that the entire tetralogy has been moving toward — the city of Chartres and its cathedral, which becomes for Huysmans the supreme object of the Catholic aesthetic imagination. Durtal comes to Chartres specifically to study the cathedral's iconographic program — the stained glass, the sculpture, the architectural symbolism of the Gothic system — and La Cathédrale is substantially a work of art historical and symbolistic interpretation: Huysmans devotes long chapters to the meaning of the different symbolic systems he identifies in the cathedral (the Marian symbolism, the prefigurations of Christ in the Old Testament, the symbolism of colors in the stained glass, the mystical meaning of the Gothic vault). The novel is also a spiritual autobiography: Durtal's meditation on Chartres is simultaneously an analysis of the cathedral and an analysis of his own spiritual condition, and the two come together in the conclusion — Durtal recognizing both the cathedral's supreme beauty and his own unworthiness to remain in its presence.",
    startWith: "La Cathédrale (1898) — the novel is long and substantially a work of art historical interpretation; it requires En Route as preparation and rewards some prior knowledge of Gothic architecture and medieval symbolism. The Clara Bell English translation (1898) exists in reprints; no modern English translation exists. Read as the fourth of the Durtal tetralogy.",
