    slug: 'yvette-maupassant',
    intro: "Yvette (1885) by Guy de Maupassant is a short novel (or long story) that represents Maupassant at his most characteristic — the story of Yvette Obardi, the innocent daughter of a woman who runs a high-class brothel, who discovers the truth about her mother's establishment when she falls in love with a man who is himself a client. Yvette combines Maupassant's two great subjects — the innocence that the social world destroys, and the hypocrisy that the social world maintains to disguise its corruption — in a concentrated form that has made it one of his most frequently discussed works. The contrast between Yvette's genuine innocence (she has grown up in the brothel without understanding what it is) and the corrupt social world that surrounds her is developed with Maupassant's characteristic precision and without sentiment: when Yvette understands the truth, she attempts suicide, survives, and comes to accept that there is no life available to her outside the world she was born into.",
