    slug: 'miss-harriet',
    intro: "Miss Harriet (1884) by Guy de Maupassant is a collection of stories that showcases Maupassant's art at its most refined — the title story, about an English religious spinster who falls in love with a French painter and drowns herself when she discovers he has kissed the farmer's daughter, is one of the most celebrated of Maupassant's stories and one of the fullest treatments of the combination of religious exaltation and sexual longing that he returns to throughout his work. The collection also includes 'Idylle,' 'Le port,' 'Mes vingt-cinq jours,' and other stories that demonstrate the full range of Maupassant's short story art: the precise observation of the provincial milieu, the sudden revelation of the forces beneath the surface of ordinary life, and the characteristic Maupassant ending in which the irony of the situation is revealed without comment. 'Miss Harriet' is also one of the most sympathetic of Maupassant's treatments of female characters — where many of his stories treat women with a combination of desire and contempt, Miss Harriet is treated with genuine compassion, and her religious longing and her sexual longing are presented as two expressions of the same fundamental need for transcendence that her sterile life has denied her.",
