    slug: 'la-debacle-zola',
    intro: "La Débâcle (1892) by Émile Zola is the nineteenth novel in the Rougon-Macquart cycle and the most ambitious historical fiction in the series — a sweeping account of the Franco-Prussian War of 1870–71 and the Paris Commune of 1871, following the soldier Jean Macquart and his friend Maurice Levasseur through the catastrophic French defeat at Sedan, the siege of Paris, and the bloody repression of the Commune by the Versailles government. Zola researched La Débâcle with his usual systematic thoroughness, visiting the battlefield of Sedan, interviewing veterans, and reading every available account of the war and the Commune; the result is the most comprehensive literary account of these events in the French language, and one of the great war novels of the nineteenth century. The central relationship — between Jean, the solid peasant soldier, and Maurice, the educated bourgeois volunteer who becomes a Communard — is one of the most carefully analyzed male friendships in French fiction, and its resolution (Jean killing Maurice during the Versailles repression of the Commune) is one of the most powerful scenes in the Rougon-Macquart cycle.",
    startWith: "La Débâcle (1892) — the novel is long (about 550 pages) but accessible and reads like a continuous narrative despite its panoramic scope; the Leonard Tancock Penguin Classics translation is the best English version. It can be read independently of the other Rougon-Macquart novels, but benefits from reading after Germinal and Nana to understand the social conditions that made the Second Empire's collapse inevitable.",
