    slug: 'le-docteur-pascal',
    intro: "Le Docteur Pascal (1893) by Émile Zola is the twentieth and final novel of the Rougon-Macquart cycle — the work that closes the series with an explicit retrospective of the entire project. Doctor Pascal Rougon has spent his life studying the hereditary history of his own family, the Rougon-Macquarts, and has amassed files on every branch of the family covering the entire period of the Second Empire. When his niece Clotilde comes to live with him, their relationship develops into a love affair that produces a child — a final act of generosity and affirmation in the face of death and hereditary ruin. Le Docteur Pascal is simultaneously the novel that closes the Rougon-Macquart narrative and Zola's explicit statement of what the entire twenty-novel project meant: Pascal's files, which contain summaries of all the other novels and all the other characters, are Zola's own files; his theory of heredity is Zola's theory; and his final affirmation of life and creation against death and despair is Zola's final statement about the purpose of his life's work. The love between Pascal and Clotilde (who is nearly forty years his junior) is deliberately scandalous — Zola was himself in a late-life relationship with a younger woman, Jeanne Rozerot — and the child born at the end of the novel is an act of defiance against the hereditary fatalism of the rest of the cycle.",
    startWith: "Le Docteur Pascal (1893) — the novel is moderate length (about 350 pages) and can be read as a self-contained meditation on the themes of the Rougon-Macquart cycle; but it rewards reading last, after at least the most famous novels of the cycle. The Helen Burnham English translation exists in reprints; no recent complete English translation exists. The Pléiade Zola edition is the definitive French text.",
