    intro: "Dix années d'exil (Ten Years of Exile, published 1821, posthumously) by Germaine de Staël is the memoir of de Staël's long exile from France under Napoleon — the most immediate and politically charged of her works, written partly during the exile itself and partly in retrospect, describing her flight from Napoleon's police in 1812, her journey through Russia just before Napoleon's invasion, and her eventual arrival in England. De Staël died in 1817, before she could complete the work, and it was published posthumously by her son. The surviving portions of Ten Years of Exile cover the period from 1800 to 1812 and include some of de Staël's most vivid prose: the description of her salon at Coppet and the European intellectuals who gathered there, the account of her flight from French agents, the extraordinary portrait of Russia in 1812 (she arrived in Moscow days before Napoleon's forces), and the description of London in 1813. As a document of the Napoleonic period seen from the perspective of its most distinguished political exile, Ten Years of Exile is also one of the most important historical memoirs of the early nineteenth century.",
