    slug: 'genie-du-christianisme',
    intro: "Le Génie du christianisme (The Genius of Christianity, 1802) by François-René de Chateaubriand is the work that created Chateaubriand's public reputation and set the terms for French literary Romanticism — a vast apology for Christianity as the source of all beauty in Western art and literature, published in April 1802 on the same day as Napoleon's Concordat with the papacy restored official Catholicism to France. The timing was deliberate and the effect was enormous: the Génie du christianisme argued, at the precise moment when a generation was emerging from the anti-religious violence of the Revolution, that Christianity was not the superstition that the Enlightenment had diagnosed but the aesthetic and spiritual ground of European civilization — the source of Gothic architecture, sacred music, medieval chivalry, classical tragedy, and the great literature of the seventeenth century. Chateaubriand's argument was as much aesthetic as theological: he claimed that Christian art was superior to pagan art because Christianity introduced concepts of infinite longing, moral suffering, and the soul's aspiration toward the divine that the finite perfection of classical art could not accommodate. Within the Génie, Chateaubriand embedded Atala and René as examples of the kind of literature that Christian sentiment made possible — they appeared first within this larger apologetic work before being extracted and published separately.",
    startWith: "Le Génie du christianisme (1802) — the complete work is very long (five parts, about 800 pages in most editions); most readers approach it through the sections that were most influential: Part Two (Poetry and the Arts), Part Three (Fine Arts and Literature), and the embedded Atala and René. The Pléiade edition is the standard French text; no recent complete English translation exists, though Thomas Wentwoth Higginson's Victorian translation is available. Read Atala and René first, then read the surrounding chapters of the Génie to understand the theological and aesthetic argument they were written to illustrate.",
