    slug: 'stello-vigny',
    intro: "Stello (1832) by Alfred de Vigny is the companion prose work to Chatterton and Servitude et grandeur militaires — the first of a planned trilogy of works about 'les souffrances des âmes extraordinaires' (the sufferings of extraordinary souls) that would examine the fate of the poet, the soldier, and the philosopher in modern society. Stello is structured as a dialogue between the narrator (called 'le docteur Noir,' the Black Doctor) and Stello, a young poet who asks whether poets should align themselves with any of the political powers of their time (the monarchy, the republic, or Bonapartism). The Black Doctor's answer — told through three historical tales of poets destroyed by each of the three political systems — is a radical aesthetic pessimism: the poet's condition is incompatible with every political system because all political systems need useful citizens and the poet is defined by his uselessness. The three historical tales are about Thomas Chatterton (the same subject as Vigny's later play), Nicholas Gilbert (a minor French poet who died in a charitable hospital during the Revolution), and André Chénier (the greatest French poet of the Revolutionary period, guillotined in 1794). Stello is the book in which Vigny states his political aesthetic most directly: the poet must be completely independent of all political power, because alignment with power always destroys authentic creation.",
    startWith: "Stello (1832) — the book is moderate length (about 250 pages) and the most philosophically direct of Vigny's prose works. Read after Cinq-Mars (1826) to understand the development of Vigny's thought from historical fiction to philosophical dialogue, and before Chatterton (1835) to understand the ideas that the play dramatizes. The Garnier-Flammarion French edition is the best text; there is an old English translation and some modern reprints.",
