    slug: 'graziella-lamartine',
    intro: "Graziella (1852) by Alphonse de Lamartine is the autobiographical novel that gives narrative form to the emotion that the Méditations poétiques lyricized — the story of a young French student's love affair with the granddaughter of a Neapolitan fisherman, set during Lamartine's youthful journey to Italy. The Graziella story is based on a real episode in Lamartine's life (he traveled to Naples in 1811–12, aged twenty-one, and formed a relationship with a young woman of the local fishing community), though the degree of fictionalization in the 1852 version is considerable. The novel is the most directly Mediterranean of French Romantic texts — its Italian landscape (the Bay of Naples, the island of Procida, the fishing villages of the coast) is rendered with the lyric precision that Lamartine brought to the Berry landscape in the Méditations; and the relationship between the French student and the Italian girl is the most perfectly achieved version of the Romantic archetype of the innocent natural woman and the educated man who cannot stay. Graziella is also important for its influence: George Sand cited it as a formative work; Turgenev thought it the most beautiful French prose of the century; and Flaubert's Sentimental Education is partly a response to the Graziella story's sentimentalism.",
    startWith: "Graziella (1852) — the novel is short (about 170 pages) and reads very fast. The best edition is the GF-Flammarion French text; English translations exist in old Bohn's Library reprints. Read after or alongside the Méditations poétiques to understand the relationship between Lamartine's lyric poetry and his autobiographical fiction; the Italian landscape of Graziella is the narrative version of the Mediterranean natural world that the Méditations invoke.",
