    slug: 'belinda-edgeworth',
    intro: "Belinda (1801) by Maria Edgeworth is one of the most important novels of the late eighteenth and early nineteenth century — a long novel of female education, social comedy, and moral testing in which Belinda Portman, a young woman taken to London by her match-making aunt Mrs. Stanhope, navigates the fashionable world and a series of morally compromised figures (the brilliant but dangerous Lady Delacour, the rakish but reformable Clarence Hervey, the colonial settler's daughter Virginia) before arriving at a mature, rational choice of her partner. Edgeworth is the most explicitly didactic of the great eighteenth-century women novelists, and Belinda is structured as a demonstration of her thesis (from her father's educational programme) that rational, principled women can resist the corrupting influence of fashionable society and make good choices. But the novel is also genuinely funny, psychologically acute (particularly in its portrait of Lady Delacour, one of the great female characters of the period — brilliant, self-destructive, and eventually redeemed), and important for its treatment of race and colonialism (the Virginia subplot) and of gender-nonconformity (the cross-dressing Harriot Freke). Jane Austen listed Belinda, with Cecilia and Camilla, among the great English novels in the famous defence of fiction in Northanger Abbey.",
    startWith: "Belinda (1801) — the Oxford World's Classics edition edited by Kathryn Kirkpatrick (1994; 2008) is the standard scholarly edition with an excellent introduction tracing Edgeworth's didactic method and the novel's reception. Reading Cecilia and Camilla (the Burney novels Austen cited alongside Belinda) first provides context for Edgeworth's position in the tradition.",
    books: [
      { title: "Belinda (Oxford World's Classics)", author: 'Maria Edgeworth (ed. Kathryn Kirkpatrick)', year: 2008, note: "The standard scholarly edition — Kirkpatrick's introduction is the definitive account of Edgeworth's didactic method, her treatment of race and colonialism in the Virginia subplot, and the novel's place in the history of women's fiction." },
      { title: 'Castle Rackrent', author: 'Maria Edgeworth', year: 1800, note: "Edgeworth's most celebrated work — the short novel narrated by old Thady about the decline of the Rackrent family is the founding text of the regional novel and the Anglo-Irish novel; read alongside Belinda to see the range of Edgeworth's ambition." },
      { title: 'The Female Quixote', author: 'Charlotte Lennox', year: 1752, note: "The predecessor in the tradition of the satirical novel of female education — Arabella's romance delusions in The Female Quixote are the comic extreme of the problem Edgeworth is addressing in Belinda; reading them together traces the tradition." },
      { title: 'Cecilia', author: 'Fanny Burney', year: 1782, note: "Burney's masterpiece and one of the novels Austen cited alongside Belinda — Cecilia's navigation of fashionable society under three inadequate guardians is the immediate predecessor to Belinda's similar situation; the two novels are natural companions." },
      { title: 'Practical Education', author: 'Maria Edgeworth and Richard Lovell Edgeworth', year: 1798, note: "The theoretical context for Belinda — the educational programme that Maria developed with her father, which informs every didactic choice the novel makes; reading even the introduction gives the framework within which Belinda's choices are designed to operate." },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What is Belinda by Maria Edgeworth about?', a: "Belinda (1801) by Maria Edgeworth follows Belinda Portman, a young woman taken to fashionable London by her match-making aunt Mrs. Stanhope, where she navigates a series of morally complex figures: the brilliant and self-destructive Lady Delacour, the rakish but reformable Clarence Hervey, and various fortune-hunters and fashionable fools. The novel is an extended demonstration of Edgeworth's educational thesis — that a rational, principled woman can resist fashionable corruption and make good choices — but it is also psychologically acute, funny, and unusual in its treatment of race, colonialism, and gender-nonconformity." },
      { q: "What is Belinda's relationship to Jane Austen?", a: "Jane Austen cited Belinda, along with Cecilia and Camilla (both by Fanny Burney), in the famous defence of fiction in Northanger Abbey as examples of great English novels: 'work in which the greatest powers of the mind are displayed, in which the most thorough knowledge of human nature, the happiest delineation of its varieties, the liveliest effusions of wit and humour are conveyed to the world in the best-chosen language.' Edgeworth's influence on Austen is more pervasive than this single citation suggests — Austen shared Edgeworth's interest in the rational woman navigating a corrupt social world, in the contrast between sound judgment and social pressure, and in the comedy of fashionable manners." },
    ],
