    intro: "The Mill on the Floss (1860) by George Eliot is the most autobiographical of her novels — the story of Maggie Tulliver, a passionate and intelligent girl growing up in provincial England in the 1820s-30s, who is trapped between her love for her brother Tom (who is everything she is not: practical, conventional, cold, successful) and her own hunger for a life that the world around her cannot offer to a girl of her class and period. Eliot wrote the novel at the same time she wrote The Mill on the Floss, and it draws directly on her own experience of growing up in the English Midlands, of her father and brother's incomprehension, of the provincial social world that was both everything she knew and everything she had to leave. The flood that ends the novel — in which Maggie and Tom die reconciled, Maggie's arms around her brother's neck — is one of the most controversial and most analyzed endings in Victorian fiction: some readers find it a capitulation, others a transfiguration.",
