    intro: "Adam Bede (1859) by George Eliot is her first full-length novel and the one that made her reputation — a pastoral tragedy set in the English Midlands in 1799, organized around four people: Adam Bede (the carpenter whose integrity and labor is Eliot's ideal of English working-class character), Hetty Sorrel (the beautiful, vain girl who becomes pregnant by the young squire Arthur Donnithorne and is condemned to death for infanticide), Arthur Donnithorne (the young squire whose weakness and self-deception have catastrophic consequences for Hetty), and Dinah Morris (the Methodist preacher whose love for Adam provides the novel's resolution). Adam Bede established Eliot's reputation as the greatest English novelist of her generation — Dickens, writing to his friend (she wrote under a pseudonym) to congratulate her, said it was 'the finest thing I have read since Thackeray.' The novel's power comes from its combination of the pastoral world (Eliot based the Loamshire setting on her own Warwickshire childhood) with the tragic investigation of moral responsibility and self-deception.",
