    slug: 'silas-marner-eliot',
    intro: "Silas Marner (1861) by George Eliot is the most concentrated and most fable-like of her novels — the story of a linen weaver named Silas Marner who is driven out of his Nonconformist community by a false accusation of theft, settles in the village of Raveloe in the English Midlands, and becomes a miser whose only relationship to the world is through the gold he accumulates. When his gold is stolen and a golden-haired orphan child crawls in out of the snow to take its place, Silas Marner is gradually restored to the human community through the love of the child (Eppie). Eliot wrote Silas Marner quickly, after The Mill on the Floss, as a 'relief' from the longer work; it is organized almost as a formal parable of the replacement of money by love, of isolation by community, of past by present. The Godfrey Cass plot (the young squire who is Eppie's unknown father) provides a realistic counterpoint to the fable-like quality of Silas's story, and Eliot uses the contrast between Silas's restoration and Godfrey's trapped respectability to investigate the relationship between honesty, community, and the possibility of happiness.",
    startWith: "Silas Marner (1861) — the complete short novel. Best George Eliot reading order: Adam Bede → The Mill on the Floss → Silas Marner → Middlemarch → Daniel Deronda.",
