    slug: 'death-in-venice-mann',
    intro: "Death in Venice (1912) by Thomas Mann is the most perfect and most disturbing of his novellas — the story of Gustav von Aschenbach, a writer of the highest reputation who has achieved his eminence through iron discipline and the suppression of everything in himself that is not dedicated to work, who travels to Venice and falls into a catastrophic passion for a Polish boy named Tadzio. The novella is organized around the myth of Dionysus and Apollo: Aschenbach has lived entirely in the Apollonian mode (discipline, form, control, clarity) and the passion for Tadzio is the Dionysian irruption that destroys him. He knows there is a cholera epidemic in Venice; he stays anyway; he follows Tadzio through the city; he dies at the beach, watching Tadzio in the water. Mann uses classical mythology (the opening vision of the exotic bird; the figure of Hermes; the god in the sea at the end), the Platonic dialogues on beauty, and the Apollonian/Dionysian opposition he found in Nietzsche to give the story its extraordinary density. Death in Venice is one of the most analyzed short works in twentieth-century literature.",
