    slug: 'faust-goethe',
    intro: "Faust (Part One 1808, Part Two 1832) by Johann Wolfgang von Goethe is the greatest work in the German literary tradition — the philosopher Heinrich Faust, who has mastered every branch of human knowledge and found it inadequate, makes a wager with Mephistopheles: the Devil will serve him in this world if Faust will serve the Devil in the next. Mephistopheles believes he can make Faust surrender to a moment of pure satisfaction — 'If to the moment I should say, Abide, you are so fair' — and Faust believes no such moment will come while he lives. Part One, which is the part most often performed, tells the story of Faust, Gretchen, and her destruction; Part Two (rarely staged, and completing its final revisions on the day Goethe died) is a vast, strange, allegorical journey through classical antiquity, the medieval court of the Emperor, the reunion with Helen of Troy, and Faust's final blinded labor. Faust is the quintessential German Bildungsroman — the story of striving, the Faustian bargain, the impossibility of satisfaction — and it has haunted Western literature since its publication.",
