    slug: 'she-came-to-stay-beauvoir',
    intro: "She Came to Stay (1943) by Simone de Beauvoir is the first and finest of her novels — a roman à clef about the famous Sartre-Beauvoir-Olga triangle, in which Françoise (based on Beauvoir) and Pierre (based on Sartre) invite Xavière (based on Olga Kosakiewicz) into their life, and Xavière gradually becomes the third point in a triangle that destroys the couple's certainty about themselves and their relationship. Beauvoir uses the novel to dramatize the existentialist concept of the Other: Xavière's consciousness is opaque to Françoise, and her mere existence as a separate consciousness constitutes a challenge to Françoise's sense of herself as a free subject. The novel ends with Françoise turning on the gas in Xavière's room — killing her to affirm her own existence as a free being. Beauvoir said the novel was her philosophical education: she used the fiction to think through the problems of intersubjectivity that Sartre was investigating simultaneously in Being and Nothingness.",
