    slug: 'the-mandarins-beauvoir',
    intro: "The Mandarins (1954) by Simone de Beauvoir is the novel that won the Prix Goncourt and is the finest of her fiction — a roman à clef about the Parisian intellectual left in the years immediately after the Liberation, in which Anne Dubreuilh (a psychoanalyst, based on Beauvoir herself) and her husband Robert (a novelist and political figure, based on Sartre) and their friend Henri Perron (a novelist and editor, based on Camus) navigate the postwar world with its revelations, disappointments, and political choices. The novel is organized around two central threads: the love affair between Anne and Lewis Brogan (based on Nelson Algren, with whom Beauvoir had her most passionate relationship) and the political struggles of the postwar left — whether to expose the Soviet labor camps at the risk of weakening the French Communist Party; whether political commitment is compatible with artistic integrity; what the intellectual's relationship to political power should be. Beauvoir said the novel was her most ambitious work and the one closest to her actual experience of the postwar world.",
