    slug: 'no-exit-sartre',
    intro: "No Exit (1944) by Jean-Paul Sartre is the most performed existentialist play in the repertoire — three people (Garcin, Inès, and Estelle) are locked together in a room that turns out to be Hell, where they will torment each other forever. The play is the theatrical demonstration of Sartre's principle that 'hell is other people' — not in the popular misreading (other people are hell because they are annoying) but in the technical philosophical sense: that our existence as conscious beings who are being-observed is defined by the presence of the Other, and that the hell of the three characters is that they are permanently exposed to each other's consciousness, unable to be alone with themselves or to escape the judgment of the others' gaze. Sartre wrote the play during the German occupation of France and directed it himself; the first production starred Michael Vitold as Garcin, Gaby Sylvia as Estelle, and Tania Balachova as Inès at the Théâtre du Vieux-Colombier in Paris.",
