    slug: 'the-entertainer-osborne',
    intro: "The Entertainer (1957) by John Osborne is the companion to Look Back in Anger — the play that confirmed Osborne's position as the most important playwright of his generation and Laurence Olivier as the finest actor. Archie Rice is a failing music-hall comedian playing to half-empty houses in a fading seaside variety theater at the time of Suez; his father Billy Rice (the last of the genuine music-hall comedians) is in the wings; his son Frank has just returned from military service; his son Mick is about to go to Suez; his daughter Jean has come home and watches Archie with dismay. Osborne uses the music-hall form deliberately: Archie's routines (the blue comedian's smutty jokes, the straight man's feed lines, the chorus girls, the Union Jack backdrop) are alternated with the domestic scenes, and the contrast between the performance and the reality is the play's formal strategy. Archie himself is one of the great characters in British drama — empty behind the facade, alive only on stage, and aware of both.",
