    slug: 'the-visit-durrenmatt',
    intro: "The Visit (1956) by Friedrich Dürrenmatt is one of the most perfect examples of the Brechtian parable in the postwar European theater — a dark comedy organized around the arrival of a very rich old woman in a very poor small town. Clara Zachanassian returns to Güllen, the Swiss town where she grew up, the richest woman in the world; she offers the town a billion marks on the condition that someone kills Alfred Ill, who seduced and abandoned her forty years ago and destroyed her reputation. The town initially refuses — but gradually, as they begin spending against the expected inheritance, as their shoes become new and their cigarettes expensive and their groceries appear on credit, the logic of the situation becomes irresistible. Dürrenmatt called the play 'a tragic comedy': it has the precision and inevitability of Greek tragedy, the blackness and comedy of Brecht, and a moral seriousness that refuses to let anyone off the hook — not Clara, not the townspeople, and not the audience who understands the mechanism while the townspeople live inside it.",
