    intro: "The Physicists (1962) by Friedrich Dürrenmatt is his most perfectly made play — a detective story set in a private psychiatric clinic, organized around the discovery that each of the three patients who has been committed as insane is actually a physicist, and that the apparent madness of each conceals a plan. The first murder happens before the play begins; the second murder happens at the end of Act One; the third murder at the end of Act Two. The detective (Inspector Voss) arrives, investigates, and leaves without solving anything. The psychiatrist (Mathilde von Zahnd) reveals in the last scene that she has stolen the physicist Möbius's equations and is already using them to build weapons. The play is organized around one of Dürrenmatt's Twenty-One Points to The Physicists: 'What concerns everyone can only be resolved by everyone. A story is not concluded until it has taken the worst possible turn.' The worst possible turn is always the Dürrenmatt solution.",
