    slug: 'pot-bouille-zola',
    intro: "Pot-Bouille (1882) by Émile Zola is the tenth novel in the Rougon-Macquart cycle and one of Zola's most savage satirical works — the story of Octave Mouret's arrival in Paris from Plassans and his gradual rise through the Parisian bourgeoisie by seducing the women of the middle-class apartment building at 12 rue de Choiseul where he lodges. Where L'Assommoir (1877) exposed the working class with compassionate fury, Pot-Bouille exposes the bourgeoisie with cold satirical contempt: every apartment in the building conceals infidelity, hypocrisy, domestic violence, or financial fraud, and Zola strips the bourgeois façade of respectability to show the corruption underneath. The novel is the direct prequel to The Ladies' Paradise (1883), in which Octave Mouret becomes the founder and owner of the great department store.",
