    intro: "Kazuo Ishiguro's The Unconsoled (1995) is the most formally radical of his novels — a 535-page dream narrative in which a concert pianist named Ryder arrives in a Central European city to give a concert he cannot remember agreeing to, in a world where space and time behave according to dream logic, where hallways open into impossible rooms and conversations loop back on themselves. Rejected by many critics on first publication as too long and too strange, it has been revalued over the subsequent decades as perhaps Ishiguro's most ambitious achievement — a novel about the impossibility of being present for the people who need us, about the endless deferral of everything that matters.",
