    intro: "D.H. Lawrence's Lady Chatterley's Lover (1928) is the most notorious novel in English literature for the better part of half a century — Connie Chatterley and the gamekeeper Oliver Mellors, the explicit sexual descriptions that led to prosecution under the Obscene Publications Act in both Britain and America, and the acquittal of Penguin Books in the 1960 trial that is itself a landmark in the history of censorship. Lawrence wrote three versions of the novel in 1926–1928 (John Thomas and Lady Jane is the second version); the third and final version was privately printed in Florence in 1928 and not legally published in Britain until the 1960 trial. Lawrence died of tuberculosis in 1930, two years after the book's first publication. Lady Chatterley's Lover is Lawrence's last completed novel; Sons and Lovers (1913) and Women in Love (1920) are his most fully achieved earlier works.",
