    slug: 'the-plague-camus',
    intro: "Albert Camus's The Plague (La Peste, 1947) is his most widely read and most politically resonant novel — an allegorical account of a bubonic plague outbreak in the Algerian city of Oran in the 1940s, narrated by Dr. Bernard Rieux, who organizes the city's medical response and observes the diverse human reactions to collective suffering: heroism, denial, exploitation, solidarity, and faith. Camus wrote the novel during the German occupation of France, and the plague is transparently an allegory for fascism (and for any occupying force that reduces human beings to statistics of death) — the novel is about how communities respond to an evil that is both impersonal and systematic, how ordinary people find or fail to find the courage to resist. But the allegory works in multiple directions: it is also about any epidemic, any collective disaster, any situation that reveals character through extremity. Camus's Nobel Prize lecture, given in 1957, identified the novel's central commitment — to resist, without illusion, the forces that destroy human dignity — as the writer's essential task. The Plague was the book most cited by readers and public health officials during the COVID-19 pandemic, when it briefly became a bestseller again.",
