    slug: 'the-fall-camus',
    intro: "Albert Camus's The Fall (La Chute, 1956) is his most ironic and formally unusual novel — a monologue delivered in an Amsterdam bar by Jean-Baptiste Clamence, a former Parisian defense lawyer who has abandoned his life and relocated to a city of canals and guilt, where he makes his living as a 'judge-penitent' who confesses his own sins in order to make his listener complicit in the judgment that follows. Camus wrote the novel in 1956, the year before his Nobel Prize, as a corrective to what he saw as the piety of The Plague and the earnestness of The Stranger — he was satirizing the Parisian intellectual who uses confession and guilt as instruments of power rather than of genuine moral reckoning. The Fall is Camus's most Dostoevskian work, drawing on the 'underground man' tradition, and it is his most ambiguous: Clamence's brilliant, self-lacerating monologue is also a seduction of his interlocutor, who never speaks. It was the last novel Camus completed before his death in a car accident in 1960 at forty-six, and its darker, more ironic tone has led some critics to see it as the beginning of a new phase; others see Clamence as Camus's self-portrait.",
