    slug: 'death-in-venice',
    intro: "Thomas Mann's Death in Venice (Der Tod in Venedig, 1912) is the perfect entry point to one of the great German novelists — a novella of about 90 pages that distills Mann's central themes (beauty, discipline, dissolution, the relationship between the artist and the Dionysian unconscious he controls) into a single sustained argument. Gustav von Aschenbach, a celebrated German writer of fifty who has lived a life of rigorous artistic discipline, travels to Venice for rest and is devastated by the sight of a beautiful Polish boy named Tadzio. Aschenbach cannot speak to Tadzio, cannot leave Venice, and cannot prevent himself from following the boy through the city as cholera spreads through the lagoon and he refuses to warn Tadzio's family because doing so would mean leaving. Mann structures the story around the conflict between Apollonian discipline (Aschenbach's artistic life) and Dionysian dissolution (his obsession), drawing on Nietzsche's framework from The Birth of Tragedy; the story is also a meditation on the artist's dangerous relationship to beauty and on the way that all art that matters is made under the shadow of the irrational. Luchino Visconti's 1971 film adaptation, with Dirk Bogarde as Aschenbach and Mahler's Adagietto on the soundtrack, is equally essential.",
