    slug: 'my-name-is-red',
    intro: "Orhan Pamuk's My Name Is Red (1998) won the Nobel Prize for Literature for its author in 2006 and is one of the great historical novels of the late twentieth century — a murder mystery set in 1590s Istanbul among the Ottoman court's miniaturist painters, told through multiple first-person narrators including a murder victim, a coin, a color, a horse, and Death itself. The novel's central conflict is between the Ottoman tradition of Islamic miniature painting, which renders the world as God sees it (without individual perspective, from a fixed divine point of view), and the Venetian technique of portraiture, which renders the world from the artist's individual perspective — a conflict that Pamuk uses to explore the larger tension between East and West, tradition and modernity, collective identity and individual expression. My Name Is Red is simultaneously a historical thriller, a meditation on the nature of artistic representation, a love story between the narrator Kara and the beautiful Shekure, and a philosophical novel about what it means to see. It is Pamuk's densest and most ambitious novel and widely considered his masterpiece.",
