    slug: 'in-the-skin-of-a-lion',
    intro: "Michael Ondaatje's In the Skin of a Lion (1987) is the Canadian novel most commonly described as the Canadian equivalent of the great Latin American magical realists — a richly sensory, non-linear portrait of the building of early twentieth-century Toronto through the stories of the immigrant and working-class men and women who constructed it but left no official record. Patrick Lewis, a young man from rural Ontario, arrives in Toronto in the 1920s and finds work searching for a disappeared millionaire; he enters the world of the bridge-builders, tunnel-diggers, and dye-workers who built the city's infrastructure, falls in love with an actress named Clara, and eventually with a woman named Alice whose partner, Nicholas Temelcoff, built the Bloor Street Viaduct in a chapter of hallucinatory beauty. Ondaatje's prose is lyrical to the point of near-poetry — dense with metaphor, non-linear in time, attentive to the textures of physical labor and immigrant life — and the novel ends where The English Patient (1992) begins, with Hana and Caravaggio as young characters. The two novels together constitute one of the great narrative projects in Canadian literature.",
