    slug: 'the-moviegoer',
    intro: "Walker Percy's The Moviegoer (1961), which won the National Book Award in 1962 in a surprise over Joseph Heller's Catch-22 and J.D. Salinger's Franny and Zooey, is a deceptively quiet novel about a young New Orleans stockbroker named Binx Bolling who spends his free time going to movies and searching — without knowing quite what he's searching for — for a way of being alive in the world that doesn't feel like death. Percy, a Louisiana Catholic philosopher-novelist who came to fiction after a tuberculosis diagnosis that gave him years of convalescence for reading, was interested in the condition he called 'everydayness' — the grey, successful, well-adjusted life that is somehow a form of spiritual amnesia. The Moviegoer is his anatomy of that condition: Binx goes to movies because they make the world real again, if only for an hour; he pursues 'the search' without being able to name it; he circles around his depressed cousin Kate in a relationship that becomes the novel's moral center. The novel is short (200 pages), deceptively funny, and one of the most Southern and most Catholic of American novels.",
