    slug: 'the-crying-of-lot-49',
    intro: "Thomas Pynchon's The Crying of Lot 49 (1966) is the entry point for one of the most elusive and important of American novelists — a short, concentrated, and frequently hilarious novel that compresses Pynchon's entire thematic universe (paranoia, entropy, conspiracy, the hidden order beneath surface America) into 150 pages. Oedipa Maas, a California housewife, is named executor of the estate of her former lover Pierce Inverarity and discovers, in the process of settling his affairs, what appears to be evidence of a centuries-old underground postal network called Tristero that has been in competition with official postal systems since the sixteenth century. Whether the conspiracy is real or a hallucination or an elaborate joke is the novel's central question — and Pynchon refuses to answer it. The Crying of Lot 49 is the ideal first Pynchon: short enough to reread, complex enough to reward it, and sufficient to tell any reader whether they want to commit to Gravity's Rainbow.",
