    intro: "Saul Bellow's The Adventures of Augie March (1953) is the novel that broke Bellow open — a sprawling, exuberant, Whitmanesque Bildungsroman that opens with one of the most famous first sentences in American fiction ('I am an American, Chicago born — Chicago, that somber city — and go at things as I have taught myself, free-style, and will make the record in my own way') and follows the young Augie March from Depression-era Chicago through a picaresque series of adventures: working for a bootlegger, training an eagle in Mexico, serving in the merchant marine. The novel was Bellow's deliberate break from the tight, restrained prose of his first two novels — he wanted to write with the energy and range of the nineteenth-century social novelists, with Dickens and Stendhal rather than the stripped Hemingway style that dominated American fiction in the 1940s. It won the National Book Award and established Bellow as the dominant voice of his literary generation, the model for what the American Jewish novel could achieve.",
