    slug: 'one-flew-over-cuckoos-nest',
    intro: "Ken Kesey's One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest (1962) is one of the most influential American novels of the 1960s — a psychiatric ward in Oregon as a microcosm of authority and freedom, narrated by Chief Bromden, the silent half-Native American patient who everyone believes is deaf and dumb, through whose distorted perception we witness the arrival of Randle Patrick McMurphy, the con man and gambler who chose the hospital over jail and who is determined to break the ward's suffocating order. The villain, Nurse Ratched, became one of American literature's great antagonists — a figure of institutionalized control whose authority depends on the patients' submission. Kesey, who had worked as an aide in a psychiatric ward and participated in CIA-funded experiments with psychedelics at Stanford, was writing from genuine experience; the novel is both a clinical portrait of mid-century psychiatric practice and a mythic confrontation between institutional power and individual freedom. Stanley Kubrick later rejected it as unfilmable; Miloš Forman's 1975 adaptation won five Academy Awards.",
