    slug: 'in-cold-blood',
    intro: "Truman Capote's In Cold Blood (1966) is the founding text of creative nonfiction's most serious ambitions — a reconstruction of the 1959 murders of the Clutter family of Holcomb, Kansas, by Perry Smith and Dick Hickock, two drifters who had been tipped about a safe they believed held $10,000, found nothing, and killed four people rather than leave witnesses. Capote spent six years researching the book, moving to Holcomb with Harper Lee immediately after reading the news item, interviewing everyone connected to the case, and developing a personal relationship with Perry Smith on death row that became one of the most complicated and debated authorial relationships in American letters. The book is simultaneously a true-crime reconstruction, a sociological portrait of middle America in 1959, a psychological study of two killers whose backgrounds Capote renders with novelistic sympathy, and a meditation on capital punishment. Capote called it a 'nonfiction novel,' and it created the genre — or defined it so completely that everything since is either an influence or a response.",
