    slug: 'no-country-for-old-men',
    intro: "Cormac McCarthy's No Country for Old Men (2005) is his most plot-driven and most widely read novel — a thriller set in the Texas-Mexico borderlands in 1980 in which a welder named Llewelyn Moss finds a satchel of drug cartel money at the scene of a massacre and sets in motion a pursuit by Anton Chigurh, a killer who has made a philosophical system of his nihilism, and by Sheriff Ed Tom Bell, an aging lawman who watches the violence with the helpless grief of a man who knows his time is over. The novel — and the Coen Brothers' 2007 film adaptation — gave Chigurh to popular culture as one of its great demonic figures. McCarthy strips his prose to its absolute minimum: no quotation marks, no adverbs, no interiority, only action and dialogue and the landscape. The result is one of the most purely readable of his novels, and one of the most disturbing.",
