    slug: 'we-zamyatin',
    intro: "Yevgeny Zamyatin's We (1924) is the first great dystopian novel of the twentieth century and the direct ancestor of both Aldous Huxley's Brave New World and George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four — Orwell acknowledged it explicitly, and the debt is unmistakable. Written in Russia in 1920–1921 but banned by Soviet censors and first published in English translation, the novel is set in the One State, a glass-walled city of perfect rational order ruled by the Benefactor, where citizens have numbers instead of names and every hour of the day is scheduled. Its narrator, the mathematician D-503, begins writing a log in praise of the One State and ends by experiencing — against his will — the terrifying, irrational, individuating force of love. Zamyatin wrote the novel as a satire of Taylorism, collectivism, and the revolutionary utopia he saw hardening into tyranny around him; it cost him his career and eventually his country.",
