    slug: 'one-day-in-the-life',
    intro: "Alexander Solzhenitsyn's One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich (1962) is the most famous account of the Soviet Gulag to appear in print during the Soviet era — a revolution disguised as a short novel. Published in the literary journal Novy Mir in November 1962 with Khrushchev's personal approval as part of the de-Stalinization campaign, the novel follows a single day in the life of Ivan Denisovich Shukhov, a peasant soldier sentenced to a labour camp on a fabricated charge of collaborating with the Germans after escaping from German captivity. The power of the novel lies entirely in its restraint: Shukhov's day is not the worst day, not the best — it is a tolerable day, and we watch him navigate it with the patient, minute-to-minute intelligence of a man who has learned to survive. The moral weight is devastating precisely because it is never stated.",
