    slug: 'snow-country',
    intro: "Yasunari Kawabata's Snow Country (1935–1948) is the first of the works cited when he received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1968 — a novel of extraordinary beauty about a Tokyo dilettante's seasonal visits to a hot-spring resort in the mountains of Niigata, and his impossible love for the geisha Komako and his obsession with the shadowy Yoko. Kawabata wrote the novel in sections over more than a decade, publishing it serially and revising it as he went; the result is a work that feels more like a series of exquisite impressions than a conventional narrative. Snow Country introduced Western readers to the Japanese aesthetic concept of mono no aware — the bittersweet awareness of the transience of things — and is regarded as one of the supreme achievements of Japanese literature.",
