    intro: "Natsume Sōseki's Kokoro (1914) is the central novel of the Meiji period and one of the most profound Japanese novels ever written — the story of a young man's friendship with an older man he calls Sensei, and the secret that Sensei has carried for decades. The novel's title means 'heart' or 'the heart of things,' and its subject is the alienation of the modern Japanese individual — a person formed by the Confucian tradition of loyalty and self-sacrifice who has been confronted by the Western values of individualism and freedom and can resolve the contradiction only through self-destruction. Kokoro is organized in three parts: the young man's encounters with Sensei in Tokyo, his return to his dying father in the countryside, and Sensei's confessional letter, which arrives after his death.",
