    intro: "Sigrid Undset's Kristin Lavransdatter (1920–1922) is the great Norwegian historical novel and one of the supreme achievements of twentieth-century fiction — a three-volume portrait of a woman's life in medieval Norway, from her girlhood through her marriage, her motherhood, and her old age. The trilogy was the primary reason Undset received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1928. What makes it extraordinary is the combination of its historical research — Undset immersed herself in the archaeology, theology, and social life of medieval Scandinavia — and the modernity of its psychological portraiture: Kristin Lavransdatter is as fully realized a fictional character as any in the twentieth century, and her inner life as she navigates the competing demands of love, family, church, and self-determination feels completely contemporary even in its medieval setting.",
