    intro: "Karl Ove Knausgård's My Struggle (2009–2011) is one of the most extraordinary literary projects of the twenty-first century — a six-volume, 3,600-page autobiographical novel that gave detailed fictional form to Knausgård's actual life: his childhood in Norway, his father's alcoholism and death, his marriages, his struggles with writing, his daily life with his children in Malmö. The project is explicitly named after Hitler's Mein Kampf, and the provocation is intentional: Knausgård was attempting to confront the contemporary prohibition on first-person seriousness, the sense that authentic self-examination had become impossible. The books caused a sensation in Norway — several people depicted in them sued or complained publicly — and have been translated into dozens of languages.",
