    intro: "Jon Fosse's Septology (2019–2021) is the masterwork of one of the most celebrated contemporary writers in the world — a seven-part novel told in a single long sentence that covers seven days in the life of an elderly Norwegian painter named Asle, who is also accompanied by a double named Asle who is drinking himself to death. Fosse's prose is unlike anything else in contemporary fiction: long, slow, circling sentences that move between present, past, and memory without transition or summary, organized around the experience of a deeply religious man who has found in his painting a way of expressing what he cannot say in ordinary language. Septology won the Nordic Council's Literature Prize, and Fosse won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2023, 'for his innovative plays and prose which give voice to the unsayable.'",
