    intro: "Italo Calvino's The Baron in the Trees (1957) is the first and most beloved of his Our Ancestors trilogy — the story of Cosimo Piovasco di Rondò, the twelve-year-old son of a minor Ligurian baron who refuses to eat a dish of snails at dinner, climbs a tree, and never comes down. Set in the eighteenth century, the novel follows Cosimo from his childhood rebellion through his long life in the trees — his love affairs, his reading, his friendships with bandits and philosophers, his participation in local political life, all conducted from the canopy — to his mysterious death. The Baron in the Trees is Calvino's most accessible moral fable and one of the most charming novels in Italian literature: a meditation on freedom, consistency, and the relationship between principle and life.",
