    slug: 'austerlitz',
    intro: "W.G. Sebald's Austerlitz (2001) is one of the most haunting novels of the twentieth century's end — the story of Jacques Austerlitz, an architectural historian who has repressed the memory of being sent to England on the Kindertransport as a child, and the narrator who meets him over a period of years in railway stations and museums across Europe. Sebald's method is unlike any other: long, undivided paragraphs that move from architecture to history to memory without transition, interspersed with black-and-white photographs that both illustrate and haunt the prose. Austerlitz is the culmination of Sebald's project of understanding what the Holocaust did to European memory and European space, and it is widely regarded as one of the greatest novels written in German since the Second World War.",
