    intro: "Joseph Conrad's Typhoon (1902) is his most approachable and most comic novella — the story of Captain MacWhirr, who drives his ship Nan-Shan directly through a South China Sea typhoon rather than sail around it, because he lacks the imagination to conceive of what a typhoon actually is. The comedy is organized around the contrast between MacWhirr's complete literal-mindedness (he cannot understand any communication that is not purely factual) and his complete competence as a seaman; the novella is Conrad's meditation on whether intelligence is actually necessary for virtue.",
