    intro: "Toni Morrison's Tar Baby (1981) is her most satirical and most self-consciously mythological novel — organized around the folk-tale archetype of the tar baby, which traps what touches it. Set on a fictional Caribbean island, Tar Baby follows the conflict between Jadine Childs, a Black model educated in Paris who has adopted European values, and Son, a man who has been living on the run for eight years after killing his wife. The novel is Morrison's meditation on the costs of assimilation and the question of what Black culture can mean in a world structured by white capitalism.",
