    slug: 'the-new-jim-crow',
    intro: "Michelle Alexander's The New Jim Crow: Mass Incarceration in the Age of Colorblindness (2010) is a work of legal scholarship and political argument demonstrating that the mass incarceration of Black Americans through the War on Drugs constitutes a new system of racialized social control that functions analogously to the Jim Crow laws of the post-Reconstruction South — stripping millions of Black men of voting rights, employment rights, and civic status through criminal conviction in a system that is officially race-neutral but structurally racial in its operation and impact.",
    startWith: 'The New Jim Crow',
    books: [
      { title: 'The New Jim Crow', author: 'Michelle Alexander', year: 2010, note: "The complete standalone work. Alexander's argument is cumulative; reading straight through is recommended. The tenth-anniversary edition (2020) includes a new preface updating the argument in light of the decade's developments." },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What is Alexander\'s central argument in The New Jim Crow?', a: "Alexander's central argument is that the War on Drugs — launched by the Nixon administration in the 1970s and dramatically escalated by the Reagan administration in the 1980s — has produced a system of mass incarceration that functions as a new form of racialized social control analogous to Jim Crow. The key move in her argument is demonstrating that although the criminal justice system is officially 'colorblind' — it does not explicitly target Black people — it operates in a structurally racial manner: Black men are far more likely to be stopped and searched by police (despite similar rates of drug use across racial groups), far more likely to be charged and prosecuted for drug offenses, and far more likely to receive longer sentences. The result is that a criminal conviction functions as a legal mechanism for stripping Black men of the same rights that the Jim Crow system stripped them of: the right to vote, the right to serve on juries, the right to access public housing and student loans, the right to be hired for most jobs. Alexander argues that this is not an accident but a design." },
      { q: 'How has The New Jim Crow been received and criticized?', a: "The New Jim Crow has been extraordinarily influential — it was a New York Times bestseller for more than 250 consecutive weeks, has sold more than a million copies, and is widely credited with shifting the national conversation about mass incarceration and racial justice. It has been taught in universities, adopted by social justice organizations, and cited by policymakers. The primary criticisms have come from several directions: from scholars who argue that Alexander's Jim Crow analogy is historically imprecise or that it understates the role of class (as opposed to race) in mass incarceration; from those who argue that the War on Drugs explanation is too narrow and that violent crime (not just drug offenses) drives mass incarceration; and from those within the Black community who argue that the book's focus on the criminal justice system ignores the economic forces that make poverty and crime more prevalent in Black communities. Ta-Nehisi Coates, in Between the World and Me, offers a complementary but differently framed account of racial violence in America that is less focused on the legal system." },
      { q: 'How does The New Jim Crow relate to current criminal justice reform?', a: "The New Jim Crow has been a major force in the criminal justice reform movement that has gained momentum since its publication: it helped provide the intellectual framework for organizations like the Equal Justice Initiative (founded by Bryan Stevenson, author of Just Mercy), the ACLU's National Prison Project, the Sentencing Project, and the broader coalition of organizations working to end mass incarceration. The book's argument — that mass incarceration is a form of racial control rather than simply a response to crime — has influenced the policy positions of criminal justice reformers across the political spectrum. The first edition appeared before the Black Lives Matter movement (which began in 2013 after the acquittal of George Zimmerman), but the book's framework for understanding anti-Black policing and criminalization became a major reference point in the movement's intellectual life. The tenth-anniversary edition (2020) was published at the height of the protests following George Floyd's murder." },
    ],
