    slug: 'how-the-garcia-girls-lost-their-accents',
    intro: "Julia Alvarez's How the García Girls Lost Their Accents (1991) is a standalone novel in linked stories following the four García sisters — Carla, Sandra, Yolanda, and Sofía — and their family, who flee the Dominican Republic in 1960 after their father's involvement in a plot against the Trujillo dictatorship is discovered and settle in New York. The novel moves backward in time from the sisters' American present to their Dominican past, tracing what is gained and lost in immigration across sixteen interconnected stories.",
    startWith: 'How the García Girls Lost Their Accents',
    books: [
      { title: 'How the García Girls Lost Their Accents', author: 'Julia Alvarez', year: 1991, note: "The complete novel. Entirely standalone." },
      { title: '¡Yo!', author: 'Julia Alvarez', year: 1997, note: "A sequel of sorts, narrated by various characters in the García world reflecting on Yolanda García (now a writer who has published a novel about her family, to their consternation) — recommended immediately after García Girls for readers who want to remain in Alvarez's Dominican-American world.", isOptional: true },
      { title: 'In the Time of the Butterflies', author: 'Julia Alvarez', year: 1994, note: "The four Mirabal sisters — Patria, Dedé, Minerva, and María Teresa — under the Trujillo dictatorship; the Trujillo regime is the historical backdrop for the García family's flight in García Girls, and this novel makes that backdrop the foreground; recommended for readers who want to understand the Dominican Republic the García family left.", isOptional: true },
      { title: 'In the Name of Salomé', author: 'Julia Alvarez', year: 2000, note: "Salomé Ureña, the Dominican national poet of the nineteenth century, and her daughter Camila — Alvarez's third novel and the most ambitious; shows her range beyond the Dominican-American immigrant experience that García Girls and In the Time of the Butterflies explore.", isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Why does How the García Girls Lost Their Accents go backward in time?', a: "The novel's reverse chronology — it opens in the 1980s with the four García sisters as adults in New York and moves backward to their Dominican childhood in the 1950s — is central to Alvarez's argument about immigration and memory. Moving backward allows the reader to understand the sisters as fully formed adults before discovering the world they came from; it prevents the immigrant origin story from being the whole story, as it so often is when novels about immigration move forward chronologically from the arrival. The reverse structure also mimics the psychological experience of the immigrant who must reconstruct the past from the perspective of the present: the sisters understand their Dominican childhood differently as American adults than they would have as Dominican children, and the novel's backward movement allows Alvarez to show the gap between those two understandings. The final section — the sisters' earliest childhood in the Dominican Republic — achieves a retrospective clarity that only the entire prior narrative makes possible." },
      { q: 'What does it mean to "lose an accent" in the novel?', a: "The novel's title operates on multiple levels simultaneously. Most literally, it refers to the Garcia daughters losing their Dominican accents as they become more American — the linguistic assimilation that is often celebrated as successful immigration. But the novel uses 'accent' to also refer to everything else that is lost or modified in immigration: the sisters' Spanish, their Dominican cultural practices, their relationship to family and to each other, their ways of moving through the world as women. Each sister loses her 'accent' in a different way and at a different pace: Carla assimilates most readily; Yolanda is the most ambivalently American; Sofía is the most rebellious against both Dominican and American constraints; Sandra has the most difficult time of all. The title is therefore ironic — losing an accent is not straightforwardly a gain — and the novel's reverse chronology reinforces this irony by showing what the sisters were before showing what they have become." },
      { q: 'How autobiographical is How the García Girls Lost Their Accents?', a: "Julia Alvarez has been consistently clear that the novel draws heavily on her own family's experience — like the García family, her family fled the Dominican Republic in 1960 after her father's involvement in a plot against Trujillo was discovered — while insisting that it is a novel rather than a memoir. The four García sisters are not direct portraits of Alvarez and her three sisters, and the events of the novel are fictionalized rather than documentary. Alvarez has described the novel as her attempt to understand the experience of her own family's immigration through the creative freedom of fiction rather than the factual constraints of memoir; the fictional frame allows her to imagine the inner lives of family members whose actual interior experiences she cannot know. The novel's most directly autobiographical element is the character of Yolanda — a writer, a reader, a woman for whom language is both refuge and inadequacy — who most closely resembles Alvarez's self-portrait." },
    ],
